# ctx read in a spawned task traps — only inside a spec case

compiler: batch-51 macOS binary (predates the ctx lane's A2), run from
/Users/tristan/projects/tristanMatthias/avra-services (branch `services`, tree unedited).

## Finding

It is not the queue, not a lambda, not a method, not `once`/state, not a detached task.
**Any `ctx` read in a task made by `spawn` traps `the context … is required` when the
`ctx` was set inside a `spec` case's helper; the same code as a program (`avra run`)
reads the value.** The smallest trap is `spec_min` below: a named fn, spawned, awaited.

- As a program: `./avra run ctx_spawn_lambda` (the queue repro, NOT in_tx) settles the item;
  `./avra run ../ctx_spawn_lambda_min` answers 6. Neither traps.
- As a spec case: `./avra test ctx_spawn_lambda` — the same queue program in
  `src/tests/spawned_test.av` settles nothing (`settled 0 | rows 0 | depth 1`): the handler
  traps, `run`'s `caught`/`guarded` turns that into `Start.Trapped`, the item is released and
  never acked, so the symptom is silent. `AVRA_CLOCK=real` changes nothing.
- `src/tests/shrink_test.av`, no queue: a lambda called in this task passes; spawning a named
  fn reading `ctx.n` traps (the run stops there, so the record/component/method/guard
  variants after it never ran — earlier, with "spawned directly" first, that one trapped too).

On the queue's path: `run` (packages/std-queue/src/queue.av) spawns the lanes OWNED
(`let _crew = [spawn self.lane() …]` in `Shift.manned`, a method), and each lane spawns the
handler OWNED and awaited through `select` (`let start = spawn caught(() -> each(d))` in
`Shift.attempted`, a method), under `guarded`. The fn value is stored in a component field
(`worker.each`), read into a local (`let each = self.w.each`) and called inside the lambda.
None of that is needed for the trap: one `spawn` of a ctx-reading fn inside a spec helper is.

